<para>2. Use the graph to:</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>estimate \(h(0)\) and \(h(4)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>\(h(0)\) is 80, and \(h(4)\) is about 10.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!-- Question 2 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>If \(t=4\), then \(h(4)\) equals what value?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(h(4) \approx 10\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!-- Question 3 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>If \(h(t) = 45\), then \(t\) equals what value?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The approximate solutions to \(h(t) = 45\) are \(t = 2\) and \(t = 9\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!-- Question 4 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>If \(h(t) = 0\), then \(t\) equals what value?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>There is no solution to \(h(t) = 0\) when \(t\) is between 0 and 35, which is what is shown on the graph. We don't know if the function will have a solution when \(t\) is greater than 35.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: The Meaning of Function Notation</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about connecting graphical and verbal representations of a function.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/08336f60e67c7d416294bbeff0c6da750df4db0f">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7a54bafd8b198ef12274c9fdae36bad6e6a563d5" srclang="en_us">
      https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/08336f60e67c7d416294bbeff0c6da750df4db0f
    </track></source></video></div>
</div>


<para>The horizontal intercept is also called the \(x\)-<term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">intercept,</term> or <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero</term>.</para>
<para>The vertical intercept is also called the \(y\)<term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">-intercept</term>.</para>

